WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.475 --> 00:00:04.503
In this final part, we will look at how

2
00:00:04.891 --> 00:00:08.488
to communicate our results clearly.
This means

3
00:00:08.568 --> 00:00:12.431
choosing suitable graphs,
indicators and scales,

4
00:00:12.511 --> 00:00:16.214
while also paying attention

5
00:00:16.294 --> 00:00:20.264
to other details,
both in speech and in writing.

6
00:00:20.344 --> 00:00:22.289
Let us take an example:

7
00:00:22.369 --> 00:00:24.927
we want to know whether water temperature

8
00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:28.468
is related to dissolved
oxygen concentration.

9
00:00:28.548 --> 00:00:31.417
We want to see whether, as one increases,

10
00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:35.051
the other decreases,
or whether they change in another way.

11
00:00:35.131 --> 00:00:39.317
We place water temperature
on the horizontal axis,

12
00:00:39.397 --> 00:00:42.391
the x-axis.

13
00:00:42.471 --> 00:00:45.048
For each temperature,
we plot the corresponding

14
00:00:45.128 --> 00:00:48.799
oxygen concentration on the vertical axis,
the y-axis.

15
00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:51.665
This gives us a scatterplot,

16
00:00:51.745 --> 00:00:54.975
as in the previous videos.

17
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:57.932
Before calculating anything,

18
00:00:58.012 --> 00:01:00.736
let us ask four questions.

19
00:01:00.816 --> 00:01:04.127
Which way does the cloud of points go?

20
00:01:04.207 --> 00:01:06.253
Upwards or downwards?

21
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:08.198
Does it follow a straight line?

22
00:01:08.278 --> 00:01:10.875
A curve, or some other shape?

23
00:01:10.955 --> 00:01:13.161
We can also assess
the association's strength.

24
00:01:13.241 --> 00:01:15.391
In other words,

25
00:01:15.483 --> 00:01:18.399
do the points lie close
to a common pattern,

26
00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:21.645
or are they spread out?

27
00:01:21.725 --> 00:01:24.990
This recalls the variance and standard
deviation discussed earlier.

28
00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:29.085
Can we distinguish groups
or unusual observations?

29
00:01:29.165 --> 00:01:31.732
We have also considered
these aspects before.

30
00:01:31.812 --> 00:01:33.629
What can we see here?

31
00:01:33.709 --> 00:01:35.826
Dissolved oxygen tends to decrease

32
00:01:35.906 --> 00:01:38.303
as water temperature rises.

33
00:01:38.395 --> 00:01:42.240
We call this a negative
correlation or association.

34
00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.893
A “negative association” does
not mean there is no association.

35
00:01:45.973 --> 00:01:50.309
It means that here,
as temperature rises, oxygen decreases.

36
00:01:50.389 --> 00:01:52.926
By contrast, with a positive association,

37
00:01:53.006 --> 00:01:55.815
temperature and oxygen
would increase together.

38
00:01:55.895 --> 00:01:59.195
Be careful: we are only describing what we

39
00:01:59.275 --> 00:02:02.655
observe. We are not saying

40
00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:06.584
that every rise in temperature causes
exactly the same drop in oxygen.

41
00:02:06.664 --> 00:02:08.240
That would be too strong a claim.

42
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.385
For now, we are describing the pattern:

43
00:02:11.465 --> 00:02:14.775
as temperature rises,
concentration decreases.

44
00:02:15.131 --> 00:02:18.232
Our points come from several catchments.

45
00:02:18.312 --> 00:02:22.693
We use different colours
to distinguish them.

46
00:02:22.773 --> 00:02:25.037
This is our third variable:

47
00:02:25.117 --> 00:02:29.995
the catchment of origin,
North, East, South or West.

48
00:02:30.075 --> 00:02:33.121
As explained earlier, we also add

49
00:02:33.201 --> 00:02:35.744
shapes so that the information remains

50
00:02:35.824 --> 00:02:38.595
accessible to people who
struggle to distinguish colours.

51
00:02:38.675 --> 00:02:40.386
Make this a habit:

52
00:02:40.466 --> 00:02:43.315
use shapes as well as colours.

53
00:02:43.395 --> 00:02:46.065
Avoid including too many groups, though.

54
00:02:46.145 --> 00:02:50.237
With four groups, the legend stays clear.

55
00:02:50.317 --> 00:02:54.472
With fifteen groups,
facets would be difficult to use.

56
00:02:54.552 --> 00:02:56.776
But with four, they work well.

57
00:02:56.856 --> 00:02:59.703
We can distinguish each catchment.

58
00:02:59.783 --> 00:03:04.248
Facets are therefore suitable here.

59
00:03:04.328 --> 00:03:07.113
They avoid putting all

60
00:03:07.193 --> 00:03:10.431
the points on one graph.

61
00:03:10.715 --> 00:03:13.876
This makes the graph easier to read.

62
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.682
We can then see more easily whether

63
00:03:17.762 --> 00:03:21.546
the observed relationship
is stronger or more

64
00:03:21.626 --> 00:03:25.352
visible in one catchment than another.

65
00:03:25.432 --> 00:03:29.677
Here, in every catchment, temperature

66
00:03:29.757 --> 00:03:34.255
is negatively associated
with oxygen concentration.

67
00:03:34.755 --> 00:03:38.165
It is essential to keep

68
00:03:38.245 --> 00:03:42.236
the same axis limits in every facet.

69
00:03:42.316 --> 00:03:45.884
Identical slopes could
look different because

70
00:03:45.964 --> 00:03:49.796
of the scale. We want to avoid axis limits

71
00:03:49.876 --> 00:03:53.656
that mislead the reader.

72
00:03:53.736 --> 00:03:55.983
Why can we not

73
00:03:56.063 --> 00:03:58.759
immediately claim causality?

74
00:03:58.851 --> 00:04:02.116
Because other factors may play a part

75
00:04:02.196 --> 00:04:06.101
in the relationship between
temperature and dissolved oxygen.

76
00:04:06.181 --> 00:04:09.250
A simple example is the season:

77
00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.267
it can affect both

78
00:04:11.347 --> 00:04:13.333
temperature and oxygen.

79
00:04:13.413 --> 00:04:15.744
Catchments may also have

80
00:04:15.824 --> 00:04:18.401
different depths or flow rates.

81
00:04:18.481 --> 00:04:22.023
The observation protocol may also produce

82
00:04:22.115 --> 00:04:24.281
patterns in the data.

83
00:04:24.361 --> 00:04:28.885
Season may therefore be
a confounding variable.

84
00:04:28.965 --> 00:04:32.703
Other factors, such as flow,
may play the same role.

85
00:04:32.783 --> 00:04:35.847
A problem with the protocol may

86
00:04:35.927 --> 00:04:38.935
also produce an artificial correlation

87
00:04:39.015 --> 00:04:42.215
with no environmental meaning.

88
00:04:42.307 --> 00:04:46.119
To establish causality,

89
00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:50.360
we need to consider other mechanisms:

90
00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:54.543
the order of events,
other variables that might explain

91
00:04:54.623 --> 00:04:58.725
the relationship, and the study design.

92
00:04:58.805 --> 00:05:02.153
A graph helps us identify a question,

93
00:05:02.233 --> 00:05:06.183
but it is not enough
to establish causality.

94
00:05:07.267 --> 00:05:10.793
Another visual

95
00:05:10.873 --> 00:05:15.143
problem occurs when
several observations lie

96
00:05:15.223 --> 00:05:19.492
in almost the same place.

97
00:05:19.572 --> 00:05:24.186
Here again, we plot dissolved
oxygen against temperature.

98
00:05:24.266 --> 00:05:26.704
We will then see what

99
00:05:26.784 --> 00:05:29.703
happens when we round the temperatures.

100
00:05:30.179 --> 00:05:32.786
This is known as “overplotting”.

101
00:05:32.866 --> 00:05:35.034
It means that the points

102
00:05:35.114 --> 00:05:37.428
overlap one another.

103
00:05:37.508 --> 00:05:40.750
We may think there are only a few points,

104
00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:44.317
when some of them hide
dozens of observations.

105
00:05:44.397 --> 00:05:47.444
To improve readability,

106
00:05:47.524 --> 00:05:51.335
we can make the points
smaller and add transparency.

107
00:05:51.683 --> 00:05:55.775
Where points overlap,

108
00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:59.552
transparency produces a darker colour,

109
00:05:59.632 --> 00:06:04.175
revealing the overlap.

110
00:06:04.255 --> 00:06:07.952
A darker point may therefore

111
00:06:08.032 --> 00:06:12.711
represent several observations
in the same place.

112
00:06:13.663 --> 00:06:17.186
Let us take another,
slightly harder example.

113
00:06:17.266 --> 00:06:20.451
If we round temperatures

114
00:06:20.531 --> 00:06:24.392
to whole numbers,

115
00:06:24.472 --> 00:06:27.994
the points overlap even more.

116
00:06:28.074 --> 00:06:30.922
Another solution is to use

117
00:06:31.002 --> 00:06:34.581
a density grid.
This shows the distribution more

118
00:06:34.661 --> 00:06:38.130
clearly than thousands
of overlapping opaque points.

119
00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:41.268
The more points a cell contains,

120
00:06:41.348 --> 00:06:46.057
the more intense its colour.

121
00:06:46.137 --> 00:06:48.183
We can also write the number

122
00:06:48.263 --> 00:06:52.269
of points inside each grid cell.

123
00:06:52.349 --> 00:06:54.804
This is much easier to read.

124
00:06:54.884 --> 00:06:56.501
It is particularly useful

125
00:06:56.581 --> 00:06:58.059
when temperatures are rounded.

126
00:06:58.371 --> 00:07:00.018
Let us move to another

127
00:07:00.098 --> 00:07:02.436
example:
changes in nitrate concentrations.

128
00:07:02.516 --> 00:07:05.397
Here, we mean changes over time.

129
00:07:05.477 --> 00:07:08.210
First, we put the data

130
00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:12.825
in chronological order:
the categories are ordered,

131
00:07:12.905 --> 00:07:14.335
January, February, March,

132
00:07:14.415 --> 00:07:16.890
and so on.

133
00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:20.001
We place time on the horizontal
axis and concentration

134
00:07:20.081 --> 00:07:24.111
on the vertical axis,
then join the measurements.

135
00:07:24.191 --> 00:07:28.359
This produces a line chart.

136
00:07:28.803 --> 00:07:32.611
The line

137
00:07:32.691 --> 00:07:36.498
guides our reading through time.
Its role is visual.

138
00:07:36.578 --> 00:07:39.800
As we have seen, it also helps us

139
00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:43.687
compare the size of changes
from month to month.

140
00:07:43.767 --> 00:07:48.001
Some values span several
orders of magnitude.

141
00:07:48.081 --> 00:07:51.249
Here, the scale is linear:

142
00:07:51.329 --> 00:07:54.631
on the y-axis, abundance increases by 100

143
00:07:54.723 --> 00:08:00.134
individuals between successive tick marks.

144
00:08:00.609 --> 00:08:03.094
On this linear axis,

145
00:08:03.174 --> 00:08:06.493
the small values appear compressed.

146
00:08:06.573 --> 00:08:08.802
It is difficult to see the differences

147
00:08:08.882 --> 00:08:11.896
between sites A, B and C.

148
00:08:12.362 --> 00:08:14.716
We can instead use

149
00:08:14.796 --> 00:08:17.388
a logarithmic scale.

150
00:08:17.468 --> 00:08:22.137
Here, the values 1,
3 and 9 are hard to distinguish.

151
00:08:22.217 --> 00:08:25.284
On a logarithmic scale,

152
00:08:25.364 --> 00:08:28.727
equal ratios occupy equal distances.

153
00:08:28.807 --> 00:08:32.230
Going from 1 to 10 on
the vertical axis covers

154
00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:36.029
the same distance as going from 10 to 100.

155
00:08:36.109 --> 00:08:39.790
It is also the same distance
as going from 100 to 1,000.

156
00:08:40.661 --> 00:08:43.210
Differences between the points

157
00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:46.375
become easier to see.

158
00:08:46.455 --> 00:08:50.614
We can now clearly distinguish 1, 3 and 9.

159
00:08:50.694 --> 00:08:53.082
This scale is useful

160
00:08:53.162 --> 00:08:55.764
for multiplicative phenomena.

161
00:08:55.844 --> 00:08:58.447
But we need to state that we are using it,

162
00:08:58.527 --> 00:09:01.773
so that we do not mislead the reader.

163
00:09:01.853 --> 00:09:04.992
If I had shown you this graph

164
00:09:05.072 --> 00:09:08.345
briefly, letting you assume

165
00:09:08.437 --> 00:09:11.375
that it used a linear scale,

166
00:09:11.455 --> 00:09:14.942
you might have thought the relationship

167
00:09:15.022 --> 00:09:18.326
between the points was linear. Yet

168
00:09:18.406 --> 00:09:21.527
the first graph showed a very rapid,

169
00:09:21.607 --> 00:09:25.049
exponential-type increase.

170
00:09:25.129 --> 00:09:27.015
Here, moving from one tick mark

171
00:09:27.095 --> 00:09:31.830
to the next means multiplying by 10.
So this is

172
00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:35.648
a base-10 logarithmic scale.

173
00:09:35.728 --> 00:09:37.704
Other bases are possible.

174
00:09:37.784 --> 00:09:40.463
Base 2 is also commonly used,

175
00:09:40.543 --> 00:09:43.456
particularly for binary phenomena.

176
00:09:43.536 --> 00:09:47.353
Here, we are using base 10.

177
00:09:47.445 --> 00:09:50.196
Remember: logarithms apply

178
00:09:50.276 --> 00:09:53.415
only to strictly positive values.

179
00:09:53.495 --> 00:09:57.713
Without going into detail,
keep this condition in mind.

180
00:09:59.957 --> 00:10:02.141
We also need to pay

181
00:10:02.221 --> 00:10:04.563
attention to missing data.

182
00:10:04.643 --> 00:10:07.827
Here, I have put the nitrate measurements

183
00:10:07.907 --> 00:10:12.091
from the West catchment in time order.
Concentration appears to

184
00:10:12.171 --> 00:10:15.987
rise until July, then fall until December.

185
00:10:16.067 --> 00:10:18.830
That is indeed the observed pattern.

186
00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:21.883
But looking at the points, we notice

187
00:10:21.963 --> 00:10:25.017
that no value is available for September.

188
00:10:25.576 --> 00:10:28.873
So we should not automatically join

189
00:10:28.953 --> 00:10:32.298
the points when a value is missing.

190
00:10:32.378 --> 00:10:35.899
Otherwise, the graph could be misleading.

191
00:10:36.399 --> 00:10:39.230
We need to show the gap.

192
00:10:39.705 --> 00:10:43.237
We should also make it clear when a

193
00:10:43.317 --> 00:10:46.963
value has been considered incorrect,

194
00:10:47.043 --> 00:10:50.402
when it is missing, or when it has

195
00:10:50.482 --> 00:10:54.415
been removed from the dataset

196
00:10:54.495 --> 00:10:57.251
because it seemed too extreme.

197
00:10:57.732 --> 00:11:00.760
We can break the line and explain,

198
00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:04.155
in the legend or accompanying text,

199
00:11:04.235 --> 00:11:07.630
why the value is missing.

200
00:11:08.126 --> 00:11:11.053
Here, only the West catchment is shown.

201
00:11:11.133 --> 00:11:14.008
We could display several
groups on the same

202
00:11:14.088 --> 00:11:17.067
graph, as long as the curves
remain distinguishable.

203
00:11:17.147 --> 00:11:21.060
We can use accessible colours,
different line styles

204
00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:25.391
or labels to identify them.

205
00:11:25.864 --> 00:11:30.681
If the curves cross too
often and become difficult

206
00:11:30.761 --> 00:11:36.045
to read, we can separate them into facets.

207
00:11:36.543 --> 00:11:40.326
To represent several
variables together, we can

208
00:11:40.406 --> 00:11:44.188
also use a graph with different axes.

209
00:11:44.268 --> 00:11:47.568
Here, the left vertical axis shows

210
00:11:47.648 --> 00:11:51.699
the number of people,
and the right axis shows

211
00:11:51.779 --> 00:11:53.704
their consumption in kilowatt-hours.

212
00:11:53.784 --> 00:11:56.261
The scales are different.

213
00:11:56.341 --> 00:11:59.821
We need to keep the curves
distinguishable.

214
00:11:59.901 --> 00:12:02.478
Here, the two curves

215
00:12:02.558 --> 00:12:05.315
are hard to tell apart, so I choose

216
00:12:05.407 --> 00:12:08.716
a suitable scale for each indicator.

217
00:12:08.796 --> 00:12:11.994
We can then see that the number of people

218
00:12:12.074 --> 00:12:17.827
runs from 100 to 180, and consumption
from 0 to 100 kilowatt-hours,

219
00:12:17.907 --> 00:12:19.828
with two clearly distinct curves.

220
00:12:19.908 --> 00:12:22.894
The legend also needs attention.

221
00:12:22.974 --> 00:12:26.019
I could have specified

222
00:12:26.111 --> 00:12:28.918
that the solid line represents people

223
00:12:28.998 --> 00:12:32.154
and the dashed line
consumption in kilowatt-hours.

224
00:12:32.234 --> 00:12:34.145
This lets us show several

225
00:12:34.225 --> 00:12:36.335
pieces of information on one graph.

226
00:12:36.415 --> 00:12:39.222
Here, we limit ourselves to two axes:

227
00:12:39.302 --> 00:12:42.557
with three or four,
the graph becomes hard to read.

228
00:12:42.637 --> 00:12:45.275
One axis on each side is enough.

229
00:12:45.919 --> 00:12:50.081
Now let us consider three variables.

230
00:12:50.161 --> 00:12:52.753
Here, we have depth

231
00:12:53.245 --> 00:12:58.583
in metres and several months,
from January to November.

232
00:12:59.057 --> 00:13:02.504
What do the values in the cells represent?

233
00:13:02.584 --> 00:13:05.981
Oxygen concentration,
in milligrams per litre.

234
00:13:06.061 --> 00:13:07.946
These values are easy

235
00:13:08.026 --> 00:13:10.112
to present in a table.

236
00:13:10.192 --> 00:13:13.186
We also indicate missing data.

237
00:13:13.266 --> 00:13:15.483
Here, “ND” marks a missing value

238
00:13:15.983 --> 00:13:20.007
in September, at a depth of 15 metres.

239
00:13:20.483 --> 00:13:23.722
As mentioned earlier, a table lets us

240
00:13:23.802 --> 00:13:26.897
look up every value.
It contains all the information,

241
00:13:26.977 --> 00:13:30.457
but does not reveal patterns visually.

242
00:13:30.537 --> 00:13:33.103
We can add colour
according to concentration.

243
00:13:33.183 --> 00:13:35.604
This gives us

244
00:13:35.684 --> 00:13:38.587
a heatmap.

245
00:13:38.667 --> 00:13:41.005
It is also sometimes
called a “colour plot”.

246
00:13:41.085 --> 00:13:45.382
Colour represents the third variable,
oxygen concentration:

247
00:13:45.462 --> 00:13:49.528
light shades correspond to low values,

248
00:13:49.608 --> 00:13:54.134
and dark shades to high values.

249
00:13:54.214 --> 00:13:57.071
This lets us visualise

250
00:13:57.151 --> 00:13:59.605
concentration across two dimensions:

251
00:13:59.685 --> 00:14:02.387
depth and month.

252
00:14:02.467 --> 00:14:04.871
Choose colours

253
00:14:04.951 --> 00:14:08.051
that everyone can interpret.

254
00:14:08.131 --> 00:14:11.131
We can also display

255
00:14:11.211 --> 00:14:15.335
the values inside the cells
to retain all the information.

256
00:14:15.427 --> 00:14:21.330
The palette depends on the meaning

257
00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:25.919
and the message we want to convey.

258
00:14:25.999 --> 00:14:27.961
For oxygen concentration, for example,

259
00:14:28.041 --> 00:14:32.125
low values can be light

260
00:14:32.205 --> 00:14:34.912
and high values dark.

261
00:14:34.992 --> 00:14:38.646
Here, concentration cannot be negative.

262
00:14:38.726 --> 00:14:42.485
If values can be negative, we can

263
00:14:42.565 --> 00:14:46.983
use zero, or another central reference,

264
00:14:47.075 --> 00:14:49.512
with a neutral colour,
here a greyish white.

265
00:14:49.592 --> 00:14:51.858
We then use two colours.

266
00:14:51.938 --> 00:14:55.118
Here, green indicates

267
00:14:55.198 --> 00:14:58.493
movement towards positive values,

268
00:14:58.573 --> 00:15:01.297
while brown indicates

269
00:15:01.377 --> 00:15:05.587
negative values.

270
00:15:05.667 --> 00:15:08.765
In a table

271
00:15:08.845 --> 00:15:12.881
or graph, the palette then shows us

272
00:15:12.961 --> 00:15:16.528
which side of the reference

273
00:15:16.608 --> 00:15:20.359
a value falls on. Here,
that reference is zero.

274
00:15:20.675 --> 00:15:22.438
This can also be useful

275
00:15:22.518 --> 00:15:24.717
for a heatmap of pH.

276
00:15:24.797 --> 00:15:27.045
We would put 7 in the centre,

277
00:15:27.125 --> 00:15:29.664
with 0 on the left and 14 on the right.

278
00:15:29.744 --> 00:15:32.331
Again, take care when choosing colours.

279
00:15:32.411 --> 00:15:35.823
For example,
we could use a rainbow palette.

280
00:15:35.903 --> 00:15:40.565
It can convey more
qualitative information.

281
00:15:40.645 --> 00:15:44.431
For example, on a hazard map,

282
00:15:44.511 --> 00:15:47.173
red may indicate

283
00:15:47.253 --> 00:15:51.431
high risk, and green

284
00:15:51.523 --> 00:15:53.827
an absence of danger.

285
00:15:53.907 --> 00:15:57.232
But colour choices always
need careful thought.

286
00:15:57.312 --> 00:16:00.297
A rainbow palette is therefore one option.

287
00:16:00.377 --> 00:16:03.930
To place less emphasis
on this qualitative,

288
00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:07.619
more subjective information, we can choose

289
00:16:07.699 --> 00:16:11.559
a more neutral palette,
like the one shown earlier.

290
00:16:12.131 --> 00:16:14.556
We also need

291
00:16:14.636 --> 00:16:17.635
to choose the indicator carefully.

292
00:16:17.715 --> 00:16:21.237
Here, we show the energy
consumption of fictional

293
00:16:21.317 --> 00:16:25.203
countries in terawatt-hours.
They are ranked from country

294
00:16:25.283 --> 00:16:28.961
A, which consumes the most, to country F.

295
00:16:29.041 --> 00:16:32.903
But this information does not

296
00:16:32.995 --> 00:16:35.758
tell the whole story.

297
00:16:35.838 --> 00:16:39.371
It is not wrong, but it is incomplete.

298
00:16:39.451 --> 00:16:42.746
If we instead show

299
00:16:42.826 --> 00:16:46.181
energy consumption per person,

300
00:16:46.261 --> 00:16:50.111
the countries' ranking
changes considerably.

301
00:16:51.779 --> 00:16:56.333
Country F consumes less energy
in total than the others.

302
00:16:56.413 --> 00:16:58.907
But its consumption

303
00:16:58.987 --> 00:17:02.463
per person is very high.

304
00:17:07.203 --> 00:17:10.556
So we need to choose

305
00:17:10.636 --> 00:17:14.040
the indicator carefully to communicate

306
00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:17.917
the information without distorting it.

307
00:17:18.799 --> 00:17:22.450
This choice depends on
our research question.

308
00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:26.654
For the overall footprint,
we use total consumption.

309
00:17:26.734 --> 00:17:29.755
When discussing fairness

310
00:17:29.835 --> 00:17:32.698
in energy use,

311
00:17:32.778 --> 00:17:36.667
consumption per person becomes relevant.

312
00:17:37.413 --> 00:17:41.451
To study production intensity, we can

313
00:17:41.531 --> 00:17:45.627
divide consumption
by gross domestic product.

314
00:17:45.707 --> 00:17:49.513
For some geographical questions,
we can examine

315
00:17:49.593 --> 00:17:53.399
energy density: consumption per km²

316
00:17:53.479 --> 00:17:57.481
or per m², in other words,
per unit of area.

317
00:17:57.573 --> 00:18:02.497
No single standardisation is
always better than the others.

318
00:18:02.577 --> 00:18:06.761
The denominator must match what we

319
00:18:06.841 --> 00:18:11.580
want to study. The unit must be clearly

320
00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:16.417
stated and allow comparisons
between countries.

321
00:18:18.181 --> 00:18:19.961
Also watch out for truncated axes.

322
00:18:20.041 --> 00:18:23.188
Take this graph of an index,

323
00:18:23.268 --> 00:18:27.016
which we do not need to define here.

324
00:18:27.096 --> 00:18:30.079
Between 2024 and 2025, it appears

325
00:18:30.159 --> 00:18:34.127
to show a very large increase.

326
00:18:34.207 --> 00:18:37.627
There is an increase, but the vertical

327
00:18:37.707 --> 00:18:41.481
axis starts at 94 and ends at 100.

328
00:18:41.573 --> 00:18:45.129
If we start the scale at zero,

329
00:18:45.209 --> 00:18:49.334
the increase is still 4.

330
00:18:49.414 --> 00:18:53.027
Visually, however, it looks much

331
00:18:53.107 --> 00:18:57.289
smaller than in the previous graph.

332
00:18:57.369 --> 00:19:00.357
A common mistake is

333
00:19:00.437 --> 00:19:03.539
to choose a scale that suits

334
00:19:03.619 --> 00:19:07.369
our message, without starting at zero.

335
00:19:07.461 --> 00:19:11.017
We need to be careful not

336
00:19:11.097 --> 00:19:14.969
to give a misleading impression.

337
00:19:16.549 --> 00:19:19.762
We can also add

338
00:19:19.842 --> 00:19:21.872
a perspective effect.

339
00:19:21.952 --> 00:19:23.801
Here, the graph has

340
00:19:23.881 --> 00:19:26.457
two dimensions, with two axes.

341
00:19:26.537 --> 00:19:29.225
We could also have shown it in 3D.

342
00:19:29.317 --> 00:19:32.913
That can be useful,

343
00:19:32.993 --> 00:19:36.860
but not necessarily here: it would make

344
00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:40.861
the increase of 4,
from 96 to 100, harder to see.

345
00:19:40.941 --> 00:19:44.537
However, 3D can be useful

346
00:19:44.617 --> 00:19:48.159
for showing three variables
together on the x-,

347
00:19:48.239 --> 00:19:52.430
y- and z-axes. Depth could

348
00:19:52.510 --> 00:19:56.457
then be used to position several bars,
for example.

349
00:19:56.549 --> 00:19:59.066
So perspective can serve a purpose.

350
00:19:59.146 --> 00:20:02.611
But in this example, it makes

351
00:20:02.691 --> 00:20:06.337
the increase from 2024
to 2025 harder to read.

352
00:20:06.757 --> 00:20:10.869
Each type of graph and each format serves

353
00:20:10.949 --> 00:20:15.517
a need related to the research question.

354
00:20:16.007 --> 00:20:19.706
A graph is not always necessary.

355
00:20:19.786 --> 00:20:23.647
Sometimes,
a short text is enough to explain

356
00:20:23.727 --> 00:20:25.556
the observed associations.

357
00:20:25.636 --> 00:20:28.526
This table summarises
several possible formats

358
00:20:28.606 --> 00:20:31.647
for different needs.

359
00:20:31.957 --> 00:20:36.016
It is not exhaustive, but it can

360
00:20:36.096 --> 00:20:40.407
help you choose formats for your work.

361
00:20:40.903 --> 00:20:44.423
Once the materials are ready,
check these final

362
00:20:44.503 --> 00:20:48.538
points before publishing the figures.

363
00:20:48.618 --> 00:20:51.932
Is the research question clear?

364
00:20:52.012 --> 00:20:55.481
Have the conclusions
been communicated well?

365
00:20:55.561 --> 00:20:59.184
Are the axes,
units and categories explicit?

366
00:20:59.264 --> 00:21:02.064
Also check the figure titles

367
00:21:02.144 --> 00:21:05.899
and legends where needed.

368
00:21:06.343 --> 00:21:08.836
Are the groups visible?

369
00:21:08.916 --> 00:21:11.410
Are missing data indicated?

370
00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:14.585
Are the units, scales

371
00:21:14.665 --> 00:21:17.980
and denominators justified?

372
00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:20.827
Are the colours accessible?

373
00:21:20.907 --> 00:21:24.660
Does the title accurately
describe what we observe?

374
00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:28.299
Above all, does our conclusion go beyond

375
00:21:28.391 --> 00:21:32.366
what the graph actually
allows us to claim?

376
00:21:32.446 --> 00:21:36.254
Your explanations must be consistent

377
00:21:36.334 --> 00:21:40.198
with the information
presented in your materials.

378
00:21:40.278 --> 00:21:42.586
Finally, a good graph

379
00:21:42.666 --> 00:21:45.753
never replaces statistical reasoning.

380
00:21:45.833 --> 00:21:49.363
It is a tool that makes
information more visible,

381
00:21:49.443 --> 00:21:53.387
so that we can check it and share it.
